WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 280: Babe Ruth: The Sultan of Swat, the Called Shot, and the House That Ruth Built

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:21.600
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the roaring, irrepressible giant of the 1920s, whose towering home runs and boundless appetite for life define the golden age of American sports.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the legendary life of Babe Ruth.

5
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:34.800
[George] Born in February 1895 above his grandfather's saloon in Baltimore, Maryland, George Herman Ruth J.

6
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:35.200
[George] R.

7
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.440
[George] was one of eight children born to German American saloonkeeper George S.

8
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:39.840
[George] R.

9
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.120
[George] and Kate Schaamberger, though only George and his sister Mamie survived infancy.

10
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:58.720
[George] Roaming the rough waterfront docks of Baltimore, skipping school, chewing tobacco, and stealing fruit from market carts, seven-year-old George was deemed incorrigible by his overwhelmed parents, who committed him to St.

11
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:05.840
[George] Mary's Industrial School for Boys, a Catholic reformatory and orphanage run by the Xaverian brothers.

12
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.000
[George] For twelve formative years, St.

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.800
[George] Mary's was young George's entire world.

14
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:21.440
[George] There, a towering 6'6 monk named Brother Matthias Boutlier noticed the boy's astonishing physical strength and spent hours hitting fungos to him on the dirt fields.

15
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:31.200
[George] Matthias became George's surrogate father, teaching him tailoring, personal discipline, and the fundamentals of pitching and hitting with a 40-ounce ash bat.

16
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:44.560
[George] The waterfront slums of Baltimore in the late 19th century were rough, perilous breeding grounds for street gangs, where young George scavenged oysters from wooden sailing schooners and drank leftover beer from baroom dregs.

17
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.960
[George] The Xavierian brothers at Sint.

18
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:58.640
[George] Mary's provided the unruly boy with the only structured sanctuary and moral love he had ever known, clothing him in blue denim uniforms and teaching him the patient trade of tailoring shirt collars.

19
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.600
[George] Brother Matthias Boutlier.

20
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:11.320
[George] A gentle giant who stood 6 feet 6 inches and weighed 250 pounds, recognized that young George's wild energy could be channeled into athletic greatness.

21
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:18.360
[George] Spending hours on the gravel diamond hitting grounders and teaching him to unleash his full torso into every swing.

22
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:30.200
[George] In 1914, Jack Dunn, owner of the minor league Baltimore Orioles, signed the 19-year-old Phenom to his first professional contract, becoming his legal guardian.

23
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:38.520
[George] When Orioles players teased the wide-eyed newcomer as Jack Dunn's newest babe, the nickname stuck for eternity, Babe Ruth.

24
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:45.880
[George] Sold that summer to the Boston Red Sox, Ruth quickly emerged as the most dominant left-handed pitcher in the American League.

25
00:02:45.880 --> 00:03:02.600
[George] From 1915 to 1918, Ruth won 89 games, posted an exceptional 2.19 earned run average, and threw 29 and two-thirds consecutive scoreless innings across two World Series, a pitching record that stood unbroken for 43 years.

26
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:10.840
[George] But Ruth's hitting was so explosive that Red Sox manager Ed Barrow was forced to transition his ace pitcher into an everyday outfielder.

27
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:23.080
[George] In 1919, Ruth shattered the single season home run record by blasting 29 home runs, an unheard of total at a time when entire teams rarely hit 20 home runs in a season.

28
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:37.320
[George] Ruth's arrival in Boston established him as the premier Southpaw hurler in the American League, compiling 23 wins in 1916 while leading the league with nine shutouts and an astonishing 1.75 earned run average.

29
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:50.800
[George] In the 1916 World Series against the Brooklyn Superbass, Ruth pitched a 14-inning complete game masterpiece, allowing only one run and holding the National League champions hitless for 11 consecutive innings.

30
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.640
[George] His conversion to an everyday outfielder in 1919 produced the most explosive offensive explosion in baseball history.

31
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:12.800
[George] Ruth hit home runs of unprecedented distance that sailed over the roofs of stadium grandstands, prompting opposing teams to erect taller fences and forcing baseball manufacturers to switch to a livelier, cork-centered ball.

32
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:26.640
[George] In December 1919, Red Sox owner Harry Frase, cash-strapped and seeking capital to finance a Broadway musical titled, No, No, Nanette, committed the most infamous transaction in sports history.

33
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:35.280
[George] He sold Babe Ruth to the New York Yankees for 100,000 in cash and a $300,000 mortgage on Fenway Park.

34
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:43.040
[George] The transaction cursed the Red Sox to an 86-year championship drought while igniting the greatest dynasty in American sports.

35
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:55.200
[George] Arriving in New York just as the 1919 Black Sox betting scandal threatened to destroy public trust in baseball, Ruth single-handedly revived the national pastime with sheer offensive fury.

36
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:06.480
[George] In 1920, his first year in pinstripes, Ruth smashed 54 home runs, outhitting every single American league team combined except the Philadelphia Athletics.

37
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:15.440
[George] Batting Averages soared, fans flooded the polo grounds in unprecedented multitudes, and Ruth became the undisputed king of New York.

38
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] The sale of Ruth to the Yankees transformed New York into the capital of American sporting culture, arriving at the exact moment that American society was emerging from the trauma of World War I and the Spanish flu pandemic into the roaring prosperity of the 1920s.

39
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:39.880
[George] Before Ruth, baseball was dominated by the inside game of sacrifice bunts, hidden-run plays, and defensive pitching duels.

40
00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:48.200
[George] Ruth single-handedly rendered that style obsolete by demonstrating that a single towering home run was worth three singles and a sacrifice.

41
00:05:48.200 --> 00:06:02.520
[George] In his first season in New York, Ruth scored 158 runs, walked 150 times, and recorded an unbelievable.847 slugging percentage that stood as a major league record for over 80 years.

42
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:12.840
[George] The massive crowds flocking to see Ruth outgrew the polo grounds, prompting the Yankees to construct baseball's first three-tiered concrete and steel cathedral in the Bronx.

43
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:30.840
[George] Opening on April 18, 1923, before 74,000 cheering spectators, Yankees Stadium was forever christened, the house that Ruth built, a fitting tribute consummated when Ruth blasted a game-winning three-run home run against his former Boston teammates on opening day.

44
00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:39.400
[George] In 1927, Ruth anchored the Immortal Murderers' Row lineup alongside Lou Jrig, Earl Combs, and Tony Lazary.

45
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.200
[George] That season, Ruth achieved the mythical zenith of baseball immortality.

46
00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:57.560
[George] On September 30, 1927, facing Tom Zachary of the Washington Senators, Ruth walloped his 60th home run into the right field bleachers, a towering record that stood unchallenged for 34 years.

47
00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:02.280
[George] The construction of Yankees Stadium was a monumental architectural gamble.

48
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:13.960
[George] Seating over 62,000 fans, it was the largest sports arena in the Western Hemisphere, featuring short right field dimensions specifically tailored to Ruth's pull-hitting left-handed swing.

49
00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:25.120
[George] The 1927 Yankees are widely considered the greatest baseball team ever assembled, winning 110 games and outscoring opponents by nearly 400 runs.

50
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:43.760
[George] The friendly home run race between Ruth and his quiet, scholarly teammate Lou Jarig captivated the nation through the summer of 1927, culminating in Ruth's dramatic late September surge where he blasted 17 home runs in the final month to secure his immortal milestone of 60 home runs.

51
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:54.160
[George] During Game 3 of the 1932 World Series at Chicago's Wrigley Field, Ruth etched his name into American folklore with baseball's most famous moment.

52
00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:05.600
[George] Facing intense razzing from the Chicago Cubs dugout and fans hurling lemons and insults, 37-year-old Ruth stepped into the batter's box against pitcher Charlie Root in the fifth inning.

53
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:15.200
[George] With two strikes against him, amid deafening boos, Ruth stepped out of the box, pointed his finger toward the deep centerfield bleachers, and dug back in.

54
00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:29.840
[George] On the next pitch, Ruth unleashed his magnificent, full-body uppercut swing, driving Root's fastball and estimated 490 feet into the temporary centerfield seats for the most dramatic home run in World Series history.

55
00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] Whether he pointed directly at the flagpole or merely signaled two strikes, the called shots cemented Babe Ruth as an American demigod who could bend reality to his majestic will.

56
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:52.800
[George] The hostility directed at Ruth during the 1932 World Series was fueled by the bitter dispute over former Cubs manager Joe McCarthy, whom the Yankees had hired to lead their pennant-winning squad.

57
00:08:52.800 --> 00:09:04.120
[George] Cubs players stood along their dugout railing waving white towels and screaming that Ruth was washed up and overweight, while fans hurled fruit and rolled up programs at him as he stepped to the plate.

58
00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:17.000
[George] Ruth's gesture toward center field with two strikes was a moment of supreme theatrical defiance, driving Charlie Roots' pitch over the deep centerfield wire barrier into the temporary temporary wooden bleachers.

59
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:25.160
[George] Ruth trotted around the bases laughing and waving his cap to the stunned Chicago crowd in an unmatched display of athletic bravado.

60
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:32.520
[George] Off the diamond, Ruth lived with an unbridled, joyous hedonism that matched the roaring excess of the 1920s.

61
00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:46.440
[George] He drove high-powered luxury roadsters at breakneck speeds, smoked 12-inch Havana cigars, and consumed gargantuan feasts of six hot dogs, two porterhouse steaks, and quarts of ginger ale in a single sitting.

62
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:57.160
[George] When told that his $80,000 salary in 1930 exceeded that of President Herbert Hoover, Ruth famously quipped, I know, but I had a better year than he did.

63
00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:02.600
[George] Yet underneath his boisterous exterior beat a heart of pure, childlike generosity.

64
00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.160
[George] Remembering his own lonely childhood at St.

65
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:12.920
[George] Mary's, Ruth spent hours visiting pediatric hospitals, orphanages, and polio wards without press fanfare.

66
00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:23.800
[George] Signing thousands of baseballs for sick children and famously promising a dying boy named Johnny Sylvester that he would hit a home run for him in the World Series, and then hitting three.

67
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:38.840
[George] Ruth's immense celebrity made him the first modern American athlete to earn millions through commercial endorsements, appearing in national advertisements for clothing, candy bars, automobiles, and sporting goods under the shrewd management of Christy Walsh.

68
00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.800
[George] His extravagant lifestyle was legendary.

69
00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:54.320
[George] He bought fleets of custom Cadillacs, gave away hundred-dollar bills to shoeshine boys, and tip hotel bellhops with gold coins, embodying the lavish, uninhibited spirit of jazz age New York.

70
00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:58.880
[George] His visits to hospital sickbeds were completely unscripted and heartfelt.

71
00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:11.520
[George] He would sit beside crippled children, hold their hands, and tell funny stories about his childhood, displaying an instinctive empathy for the forgotten and downtrodden that endeared him to working-class Americans.

72
00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:26.320
[George] Ruth retired in 1935 with 714 career home runs, a staggering 2,213 runs batted in, and an all-time record slugging percentage of.690 that has never been surpassed.

73
00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:33.280
[George] In 1936, he was elected as one of the inaugural five members of the National Baseball Hall of Fame.

74
00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:45.760
[George] In his final years, battling agonizing throat cancer, Ruth made his final, poignant appearance at Yankee Stadium on June 13, 1948, for the stadium's 25th anniversary.

75
00:11:45.760 --> 00:12:00.960
[George] Leaning heavily on a baseball bat for support, his famous number three retired on his back, Ruth gazed out at 50,000 cheering fans through weeping eyes as photographer Nat Fain captured the Pulitzer prize-winning photograph of his bowed silhouette.

76
00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:09.360
[George] Passing away two months later at age 53, over 100,000 mourners filed past his open casket at Yankee Stadium.

77
00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.400
[George] Babe Ruth was more than a baseball player.

78
00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:20.240
[George] He was the buoyant, roaring heartbeat of American optimism, proving that an abandoned boy from the streets could become the king of the world.

79
00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:32.440
[George] In his final years, Ruth longed to manage a major league baseball team, but baseball owners feared his reckless personal habits and boisterous independence, shutting him out of front office leadership.

80
00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:48.280
[George] When he walked onto the diamond at Yankee Stadium for the final time in June 1948, his frail body ravaged by inoperable nasopharyngeal carcinoma, 50,000 fans stood in weeping silence to honor the man who had brought them so much joy.

81
00:12:48.280 --> 00:13:06.680
[George] Babe Ruth's 714 home runs stood as an unassailable mountain of achievement for 40 years, but his true immortality resides in the joyous, roaring spirit he breathed into American baseball, turning a simple summer game into an enduring cathedral of American dreams.

82
00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:18.520
[Rebecca] Babe Ruth's towering home runs, childlike joy, and iconic presence embodied the roaring spirit of 20th century America.

83
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:27.480
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the revolutionary musical life of Louis Armstrong, Satchmo, the founding father of American jazz.

84
00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.120
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

